g failed first. The practical effect is only that the driver gets no exoneration, not that the rider gets a refund the distance test does not support.\n\nOn the rider's side: the trip identifiers and timestamps match across the trip record, the trace and the fare ledger, so POL-RD-08(a) is satisfied; but limb (b), an excess over threshold, is not, so the burden never shifts. The rider's own case is candid that no distance deviation is established.\n\nNo billing-integrity correction under POL-RD-09(4) is due: the fare breakdown carries no ERP gantries and no airport surcharge, and its lines reconcile to the 29.91 charged. No driver compensation under POL-RD-04 is due: the billed distance (21.8 km) is not less than the trace distance (21.79 km), so there is no unbilled shortfall. POL-RD-06 is not engaged: the excess is nowhere near material (8.0 km or 50%), and in any event the driver's 90-day record shows 0 deviation flags across 640 completed trips, so no conduct referral arises.\n\nOutcome: NO_ACTION. Nothing is refunded and nothing is held; the fare stands as charged."
